[s0l0!-header][h1]Pointers and Arrays[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Pointers are especially useful with arrays. An array declaration reserves a block of contiguous memory addresses for its elements. With pointers, we can point to the first element and then use [b]address arithmetic [/b]to traverse the array:
 + is used to move forward to a memory location
 - is used to move backward to a memory location

[s0l0!-header][b]Consider the following program:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1709"]int a[5] = {22, 33, 44, 55, 66};
int *ptr = NULL;
int i;

ptr = a;
for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  printf("%d ", *(ptr + i));
}[/code]
The program output is: 22  33  44  55  66

An important concept with arrays is that an [b]array name[/b] acts as a [b]pointer[/b] to the first element of the array. Therefore, the statement [b]ptr = a[/b] can be thought of as [b]ptr = &a[0][/b].
Consider the following statement, which prints the first element of the array: printf("%d ", *a);
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

